  "query_preview": "..\nSouthwest flow has set up across the region, pulling in tropical\nmoisture from Hurricane Marie. PWATS over Southeast Arizona are\nat the 90th percentile or higher for this early September. The\nabove normal moisture will prime the day for heavy rainfall\nrisk. The latest CAM model have confidence for this afternoon \nstorms to produce up to 1-1.5 inches with pockets up to 2 inches\nin a short amount of time. Ground conditions are primed and can\nreact quickly to heavy rainfall because of the previous 48 hour\nrainfall. Areas south of Tucson will be the most susceptible to\nquicker response in washes and creeks and heighten risk for \nflash flooding. As result, there is a Flash Flood Watch for the \neastern Pima (including Tucson metro), Santa Cruz County, and \neastern Cochise County (Benson south to Sierra Vista/Hereford) \nfor today and through the early tomorrow morning. Several models\ncontinue to show activity lasting through the nighttime and \nadding more rainfall accumulation.\n\nTomorrow, moisture will shift more east of Tucson, allowing\nCochise, Graham, and Greenlee Counties to have better chances\nfor rain showers and thunderstorms. Rainfall amounts can be an\ninch with localized higher amounts. WPC has a slight risk (<15%)\nfor flash flooding those counties. Thunderstorm activity will\ndrop around sunset. \n\nLooking into the holiday weekend. The upper pattern will\ngenerally stay the same with a closed low over the Pacific\nNorthwest and ridging to the east. Yet, the best chances for \nrain showers and thunderstorms will mainly be confined to the \nInternational Border and east of Tucson through the weekend. \nBetter widespread chances will return by next week with a \nreemergence of deeper moisture.\n\nTemperatures will be hovering near normal to slightly below\nnormal through the forecast period. \n\nThe 8-14 day CPC outlook has a 50-60% likely above normal\nprecipitation and 33-50% leaning above normal temperatures.",
